---
import Callout from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import ContentSection from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/ContentSection.astro";
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro";
import { href } from "../../lib/link";

export const prerender = true;
---

<DocsPage title="Subscriptions">
	<ContentSection label="Subscriptions">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			Subscriptions are one of the most powerful patterns in WebSocket communication.
			You send a message to the server saying "I'm interested in this data," and the
			server pushes updates to you over time. Think of it like tuning into a radio
			channel — you subscribe once, and the updates keep flowing.
		</p>
		<p class="text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
			react-socket makes subscriptions safe and efficient. Multiple components can
			subscribe to the same channel and only one message is sent to the server.
			When the last component unmounts, the unsubscribe is sent automatically.
			No duplicate messages, no leaked subscriptions, no manual bookkeeping.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="The Problem Without Deduplication">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			Imagine three components all need live price data for the same stock ticker.
			Without deduplication, each component sends its own subscribe message — the
			server sees three identical requests, allocates three sets of resources, and
			pushes three copies of every update. When components unmount and remount
			(React strict mode, navigation, dynamic lists), it gets worse.
		</p>
		<p class="text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
			react-socket solves this with ref-counting. The first component to subscribe
			sends the message. The second and third just increment a counter. Unsubscribes
			decrement it. Only when the counter hits zero does the actual unsubscribe go
			out to the server.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="How It Works">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			Each subscription is identified by a <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">key</code> string
			that you choose (e.g. <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">"prices:AAPL"</code> or
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">"chat:general"</code>). The manager keeps an internal
			counter for each key:
		</p>
		<ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-white/60 mb-4">
			<li>First <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">subscribe("chat:general")</code> — counter goes to 1, message sent to server</li>
			<li>Second call with the same key — counter goes to 2, no message sent</li>
			<li>First <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">unsubscribe("chat:general")</code> — counter drops to 1, no message sent</li>
			<li>Last unsubscribe — counter drops to 0, unsubscribe message sent to server</li>
		</ul>
		<Callout type="tip" title="Strict Mode Safe">
			React strict mode fires effects twice in development. Ref-counting handles
			this correctly: the counter goes 0 &rarr; 1 &rarr; 0 &rarr; 1, and only one
			subscribe message is ever sent to the server.
		</Callout>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Subscribing and Unsubscribing">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			Pass a unique key and an optional message to send to the server.
			The <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">data</code> argument is stored internally and reused
			to re-subscribe after a reconnect:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`// Subscribe — sends the message on first subscriber only
manager.subscribe(
  "chat:general",
  { action: "subscribe", type: "channel", channel: "general" }
);

// Unsubscribe — sends only when the last subscriber leaves
manager.unsubscribe(
  "chat:general",
  { action: "unsubscribe", type: "channel", channel: "general" }
);`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="subscriptions.ts"
		/>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Using in React Components">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			The natural place to subscribe is a <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">useEffect</code>.
			Return the unsubscribe as cleanup. Because of ref-counting, you don't need to
			worry about multiple instances of the same component or strict mode double-firing:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`import { useEffect } from "react";

function ChatRoom({ channel }: { channel: string }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    manager.subscribe(
      \`chat:\${channel}\`,
      { action: "subscribe", type: "channel", channel }
    );

    return () => {
      manager.unsubscribe(
        \`chat:\${channel}\`,
        { action: "unsubscribe", type: "channel", channel }
      );
    };
  }, [channel]);

  // Messages arrive via onMessageReceived in the manager
  // and get dispatched to your store — this component just reads from it
  const messages = useStore((s) => s.messages[channel] ?? []);

  return (
    <ul>
      {messages.map((m) => <li key={m.id}>{m.sender}: {m.text}</li>)}
    </ul>
  );
}`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="ChatRoom.tsx"
		/>
		<p class="text-white/60 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
			If <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">ChatRoom</code> is rendered five times on the page
			with the same channel, only one subscribe message is sent to the server.
			All five components read from the same store, updated by the single
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">onMessageReceived</code> callback.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Pending Subscriptions">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			When a subscribe message is sent, the key is marked as "pending" until you
			explicitly resolve it. This prevents the manager from re-sending if the
			component re-renders before the server confirms:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`// In your onMessageReceived dispatcher:
onMessageReceived(msg) {
  if (msg.action === "subscribe_ack") {
    manager.resolvePendingSubscription(\`chat:\${msg.channel}\`);
  }
}`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="manager.ts"
		/>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Automatic Restoration on Reconnect">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			When the connection drops and reconnects, the manager automatically re-sends
			all active subscriptions using the stored <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">data</code>
			payload. Your components don't need to do anything — they stay mounted, the
			manager handles re-subscribing, and messages resume flowing.
		</p>
		<p class="text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
			The <code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">onReady</code> callback fires after subscriptions
			have been restored.
			See <a href={href("/docs/reconnection")} class="text-accent hover:underline">Reconnection</a> for more.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Cleanup with onLastUnsubscribe">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			When the ref count for a key drops to zero, the
			<code class="text-accent font-mono text-sm">onLastUnsubscribe</code> callback fires. Use it to
			clean up related state — for example, clearing cached messages for a channel
			the user has fully navigated away from:
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`const manager = new WebSocketManager<TClientMsg, TServerMsg>({
  // ...
  onLastUnsubscribe(key, data) {
    // No more components are listening to this channel
    store.clearChannel(key);
  },
});`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="manager.ts"
		/>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Next Steps">
		<ul class="space-y-2 text-white/60">
			<li>
				<a href={href("/docs/optimistic-updates")} class="text-accent hover:underline">Sending Messages</a> — fire-and-forget and acknowledged sends
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href={href("/docs/reconnection")} class="text-accent hover:underline">Reconnection</a> — how subscriptions survive disconnects
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href={href("/docs/api")} class="text-accent hover:underline">API Reference</a> — full method list
			</li>
		</ul>
	</ContentSection>
</DocsPage>
